1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,180
This quaternion lecture has two episodes.
After outlining the complete journey,

2
00:00:06,660 --> 00:00:10,700
this first episode explains how to construct a
stored rotation and undo it.

3
00:00:11,350 --> 00:00:14,930
How can four numbers record an airborne
rotation in a game?

4
00:00:15,110 --> 00:00:17,930
We will observe ski and bike orientations,

5
00:00:18,370 --> 00:00:21,890
then visualize the four quaternion components
and their half-angle.

6
00:00:22,290 --> 00:00:24,670
We will undo and compose rotations,

7
00:00:25,050 --> 00:00:26,730
and compute the difference between two
orientations.

8
00:00:27,430 --> 00:00:29,810
Finally, we will check sign and normalization

9
00:00:30,050 --> 00:00:32,850
pitfalls and rotate a vector in a worked
exercise.

10
00:00:34,050 --> 00:00:37,830
Alongside skiing, we compare aircraft and
assembled components.

11
00:00:38,310 --> 00:00:40,970
Identify what changes and what remains the
same.

12
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:48,160
Our goal is to record the body's orientation
at one instant.

13
00:00:48,420 --> 00:00:51,460
Let us first distinguish where the rider is
from how the body is turned.

14
00:00:52,100 --> 00:01:00,796
This is skiing in Riders Republic. Watch the
tilt of the body and skis.

15
00:01:01,076 --> 00:01:06,176
Read forward movement separately from changes
in body orientation.

16
00:01:08,652 --> 00:01:12,772
In the air, the travel path continues while
facing and bank also change.

17
00:01:15,208 --> 00:01:20,488
Remaining near screen center does not imply an
unchanged orientation; the camera follows too.

18
00:01:23,184 --> 00:01:26,944
We are learning a representation that can
describe the visible motion,

19
00:01:27,024 --> 00:01:31,880
without claiming to have inspected the game
code.

20
00:01:31,880 --> 00:01:35,520
Our next diagram will hold position fixed and

21
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:37,480
rotate the three body axes to isolate
orientation.

22
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:41,020
To understand this approach to rotation
through multiplication,

23
00:01:41,020 --> 00:01:44,280
we first extend the number system. The square
of a real number is nonnegative,

24
00:01:44,620 --> 00:01:49,720
so no real number squares to minus one.

25
00:01:50,160 --> 00:01:54,400
We define a new rule in which i squared is
minus one.

26
00:01:54,820 --> 00:01:57,500
The number i defined this way is called the
imaginary unit.

27
00:01:57,820 --> 00:02:02,000
This rule does not make an imaginary number a
hidden object inside the game.

28
00:02:02,280 --> 00:02:05,060
It provides a useful numerical tool for
direction calculations.

29
00:02:05,060 --> 00:02:09,840
To see why it helps with rotation, we will
represent these numbers as point coordinates.

30
00:02:10,467 --> 00:02:15,587
A complex number a+bi represents a point (a,b)
in a plane. Its horizontal

31
00:02:15,787 --> 00:02:19,547
coordinate is the real part and its vertical
coordinate is the coefficient of i.

32
00:02:19,827 --> 00:02:23,487
The point with x coordinate two and y
coordinate one is 2+i.

33
00:02:23,767 --> 00:02:29,847
Multiplying by i gives 2i+i squared, which
equals minus one plus 2i.

34
00:02:30,087 --> 00:02:33,527
The new x coordinate is minus one and the new
y coordinate is two. The arrow turns

35
00:02:33,707 --> 00:02:37,447
ninety degrees counterclockwise while
retaining length square root of five.

36
00:02:37,447 --> 00:02:41,047
Multiplying a point (a,b) by i changes it to
(-b,a),

37
00:02:41,047 --> 00:02:44,747
allowing multiplication to represent a planar
turn.

38
00:02:44,958 --> 00:02:48,307
In three dimensions, we also need an axis that
defines the rotation plane.

39
00:02:48,507 --> 00:02:52,447
We will extend this direction calculation to a
quaternion using that axis.

40
00:02:53,067 --> 00:02:55,987
We verified that multiplying by i produces a
ninety-degree turn.

41
00:02:56,107 --> 00:02:58,947
How can we represent other angles?

42
00:02:59,187 --> 00:03:03,047
Recall the unit circle from high-school
trigonometry. A unit arrow turned by

43
00:03:03,047 --> 00:03:07,567
theta has horizontal component cos(theta) and
vertical component sin(theta).

44
00:03:07,807 --> 00:03:13,507
Multiplication by cos(theta)+i sin(theta)
therefore rotates the plane by theta.

45
00:03:13,767 --> 00:03:18,387
At ninety degrees the cosine is zero and the
sine is one, giving i.

46
00:03:18,587 --> 00:03:21,867
This planar calculation uses the full physical
angle theta.

47
00:03:21,867 --> 00:03:24,947
When the three-dimensional quaternion later
uses a half-angle,

48
00:03:24,947 --> 00:03:28,567
we will compare the different operation
applied to the vector.

49
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:30,740
We learned about a planar angle.

50
00:03:33,031 --> 00:03:34,551
Observe the red torso line.

51
00:03:36,806 --> 00:03:38,582
Three dimensions require an axis too.

52
00:03:40,913 --> 00:03:42,373
We now connect it to the three axis
components.

53
00:03:43,233 --> 00:03:47,353
In a plane, two numbers and multiplication
could turn a direction. Extending that

54
00:03:47,833 --> 00:03:53,133
idea to three dimensions requires choosing an
axis; let us examine the numbers we need.

55
00:03:53,767 --> 00:03:55,987
A scalar means one number. Here a vector is a
three-number group describing a direction,

56
00:03:55,987 --> 00:04:02,847
and a component is an individual number in
that group.

57
00:04:03,107 --> 00:04:06,847
The symbols i,j,k belong to the
three-dimensional rotation algebra.

58
00:04:06,905 --> 00:04:11,567
We do not need to draw a fourth physical
direction to store four numbers.

59
00:04:11,847 --> 00:04:14,807
For a z-axis rotation, x and y are zero while
w and z carry angle information.

60
00:04:14,807 --> 00:04:21,187
These are not four independent rotation
angles.

61
00:04:21,187 --> 00:04:24,307
Which values produce 90 degrees?

62
00:04:24,467 --> 00:04:27,667
We will construct them using a unit axis and
familiar trigonometry.

63
00:04:28,300 --> 00:04:32,340
A quaternion contains one scalar and a
three-component vector.

64
00:04:32,560 --> 00:04:37,180
We write components in w,x,y,z order.

65
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:40,960
A unit rotation quaternion has squared
components summing to one,

66
00:04:41,180 --> 00:04:43,720
leaving three continuous degrees of freedom.

67
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:50,560
Four numbers are not four rotation angles, and
x,y,z are not Euler angles.

68
00:04:50,640 --> 00:04:56,180
The algebra uses i,j,k, whose squares each
equal minus one.

69
00:04:56,180 --> 00:05:02,220
The product ij is k, whereas ji is minus k: an
early hint that order matters.

70
00:05:02,580 --> 00:05:08,960
A library may store x,y,z,w instead. Check the
named components and memory order.

71
00:05:09,943 --> 00:05:12,803
Read the direction represented by three
components through the wings.

72
00:05:15,391 --> 00:05:18,011
This excerpt changes from bicycle travel to
flight.

73
00:05:20,519 --> 00:05:23,199
The red line joins the visible wing tips.

74
00:05:25,707 --> 00:05:28,627
Compare it with the teal screen-horizontal
reference.

75
00:05:31,195 --> 00:05:34,515
The camera also moves, so this projected angle
is not a world rotation angle.

76
00:05:37,043 --> 00:05:41,163
How do we construct four components from an
axis and angle independent of the camera?

77
00:05:42,043 --> 00:05:47,343
We have separated the roles of the four
components.

78
00:05:47,563 --> 00:05:51,283
Now we need to construct them from the axis
and angle introduced in the preceding lesson.

79
00:05:51,917 --> 00:05:58,637
Recall a unit axis and the angle of rotation
about it.

80
00:05:59,037 --> 00:06:02,697
The scalar is cos of the half-angle,

81
00:06:02,917 --> 00:06:05,677
and the vector is the unit axis times sin of
the half-angle.

82
00:06:05,957 --> 00:06:11,577
At zero angle, cos is one and sin is zero,
giving the identity [1,0,0,0].

83
00:06:11,937 --> 00:06:18,837
A 90-degree z rotation uses a 45-degree
half-angle, so w and z are both sqrt(2)/2.

84
00:06:19,057 --> 00:06:21,537
The half-angle does not mean half the physical
rotation.

85
00:06:21,537 --> 00:06:25,997
Multiplication on both sides produces the full
angle.

86
00:06:26,237 --> 00:06:28,637
We retain right-handed column-vector
conventions,

87
00:06:28,817 --> 00:06:32,217
and use radians in trigonometric calculations.

88
00:06:32,850 --> 00:06:36,450
A planar rotation used sine and cosine of the
full angle,

89
00:06:36,450 --> 00:06:41,190
but our quaternion uses 45 degrees for a
90-degree turn. This needs an explanation.

90
00:06:41,450 --> 00:06:44,150
The difference is how we later apply it: the
rotation value multiplies

91
00:06:44,350 --> 00:06:49,390
the vector on the left, and its inverse
multiplies it on the right.

92
00:06:49,610 --> 00:06:53,510
The complete two-sided operation produces the
90-degree z rotation.

93
00:06:53,730 --> 00:06:58,350
A one-sided intermediate is not a physical
45-degree orientation of the object.

94
00:06:58,350 --> 00:07:01,170
For now, distinguish the stored half-angle
from the physical turn;

95
00:07:01,170 --> 00:07:06,750
we will later calculate both products on the
same vector to verify the connection.

96
00:07:07,383 --> 00:07:09,403
The half-angle belonged to the storage
formula.

97
00:07:09,403 --> 00:07:14,089
Observe the red torso and blue wheel lines.

98
00:07:16,975 --> 00:07:19,295
The complete two-sided operation produces
ninety degrees;

99
00:07:19,295 --> 00:07:24,521
it does not rotate the object by half.

100
00:07:27,104 --> 00:07:29,467
We now connect the four components to the
visible orientation.

101
00:07:30,167 --> 00:07:40,939
This is a Trailmakers aircraft. Inspect the
red nose direction and blue wing direction

102
00:07:41,019 --> 00:07:45,199
together. After takeoff, the aircraft banks
and later approaches level wings.

103
00:07:47,995 --> 00:07:50,695
As with skiing, separate position from
orientation: flight continues

104
00:07:50,695 --> 00:08:01,107
while orientation changes. These lines depict
observed screen directions,

105
00:08:01,107 --> 00:08:04,547
not measured world angles or internal
calculations.

106
00:08:05,167 --> 00:08:09,287
For our defined aircraft model, x points
forward, y points toward one wing,

107
00:08:09,287 --> 00:08:15,147
and z points up. This is not an assumed game
coordinate system.

108
00:08:15,607 --> 00:08:17,947
A ninety-degree turn around the forward axis
leaves the nose

109
00:08:17,947 --> 00:08:20,907
direction unchanged and moves that wing
direction upward.

110
00:08:20,973 --> 00:08:25,347
Its wxyz components are square root of two
over two, the same value, zero, zero.

111
00:08:25,607 --> 00:08:29,987
The angle matches our earlier z-axis turn, but
the axis occupies a different component.

112
00:08:30,633 --> 00:08:33,533
We have defined a 90-degree turn about z.
Returning to the game,

113
00:08:34,093 --> 00:08:37,913
we can watch body directions change together;

114
00:08:38,413 --> 00:08:42,753
the angle projected by a moving camera is not
our defined world angle.

115
00:08:43,567 --> 00:08:47,767
Compare body facing when the skis leave the
ground and when they land.

116
00:08:50,395 --> 00:08:55,655
Imagine three small body axes; all of them
rotate together in the air.

117
00:08:58,423 --> 00:09:03,843
One direction alone cannot describe both body
facing and ski bank.

118
00:09:03,856 --> 00:09:12,591
A quaternion is one way to store the full
orientation, not position or velocity.

119
00:09:15,339 --> 00:09:18,979
We are not measuring exact axes and angles
from the screen.

120
00:09:19,159 --> 00:09:24,547
We will define those explicitly in the next
calculation.

121
00:09:24,547 --> 00:09:27,247
Watch orientation separately from travel
direction around

122
00:09:27,247 --> 00:09:29,727
landing to connect the representation to
visible motion.

123
00:09:30,567 --> 00:09:39,293
An axis and angle gave us a rotation value.
Observe the red torso and blue wheel lines.

124
00:09:42,560 --> 00:09:44,920
What happens when we apply that rotation twice
about one axis?

125
00:09:48,047 --> 00:09:50,947
We now compose two ninety-degree turns into
one product.

126
00:09:54,187 --> 00:09:57,027
Inspect the orange selected Besiege parts.

127
00:09:57,127 --> 00:10:01,847
A rotation changes their directions together
while neighboring unselected parts remain.

128
00:10:02,467 --> 00:10:07,007
Applying the same ninety-degree model rotation
to two perpendicular directions preserves

129
00:10:07,007 --> 00:10:12,607
their right angle. That differs from applying
a different rotation to each direction.

130
00:10:12,807 --> 00:10:15,187
Skis, aircraft, ship parts and assembled
machines all present

131
00:10:15,187 --> 00:10:19,067
orientation problems despite their different
shapes.

132
00:10:19,347 --> 00:10:24,007
We now return to the original calculation and
apply the same rotation a second time.

133
00:10:24,633 --> 00:10:27,353
We saw the whole orientation change.

134
00:10:27,433 --> 00:10:30,753
What happens if we apply the same 90-degree
turn again?

135
00:10:31,013 --> 00:10:36,053
We will calculate a fixed-axis example rather
than measure a particular game maneuver.

136
00:10:36,683 --> 00:10:42,683
Multiply the z90 quaternion by itself,
applying the same rotation twice.

137
00:10:42,863 --> 00:10:48,983
The new scalar is the product of scalars minus
the vector dot product, which is zero here.

138
00:10:49,031 --> 00:10:51,763
The two cross scalar-vector terms sum to a z
component of one;

139
00:10:51,763 --> 00:10:54,523
the other components remain zero.

140
00:10:54,723 --> 00:10:59,483
The result [0,0,0,1] represents z180.

141
00:10:59,743 --> 00:11:02,603
After two more such rotations, the result is
[-1,0,0,0],

142
00:11:02,603 --> 00:11:06,263
with the body back at its initial orientation.

143
00:11:06,263 --> 00:11:09,823
The scalar changed from one to minus one, yet
the orientation matches.

144
00:11:10,183 --> 00:11:12,823
Let us examine this double representation.

145
00:11:13,783 --> 00:11:18,393
Four turns restored the orientation but
changed the signs of its stored components.

146
00:11:18,653 --> 00:11:22,813
We must explain that difference to compare
orientations correctly.

147
00:11:23,500 --> 00:11:29,580
The unit quaternions q and -q produce the same
rotation matrix and endpoint orientation.

148
00:11:29,740 --> 00:11:32,320
All four components change sign. This differs
from conjugation,

149
00:11:32,600 --> 00:11:35,720
which changes only the vector signs.

150
00:11:35,960 --> 00:11:40,320
In vector rotation, the signs on both sides
cancel.

151
00:11:40,580 --> 00:11:44,960
Component equality alone is therefore
insufficient to test orientation equality.

152
00:11:45,420 --> 00:11:48,460
Equivalent endpoints do not make every
interpolation

153
00:11:48,740 --> 00:11:52,120
path between signed representatives
equivalent.

154
00:11:52,120 --> 00:11:54,980
Endpoint orientation does not retain
revolution count;

155
00:11:55,320 --> 00:11:57,720
store the path and accumulated rotation
separately.

156
00:11:58,833 --> 00:12:02,183
The equality of q and minus q concerned the
ending orientation.

157
00:12:04,807 --> 00:12:06,187
Continue observing the jump and landing.

158
00:12:08,791 --> 00:12:12,371
The red torso line and blue ski line record
visible directions over time.

159
00:12:15,960 --> 00:12:19,780
One endpoint image cannot recover the
intervening turn.

160
00:12:23,429 --> 00:12:30,784
Our calculation flips all components; it does
not reverse rotation or rewind a route.

161
00:12:33,959 --> 00:12:37,079
Which value applies no extra rotation?

162
00:12:39,993 --> 00:12:42,273
We verify the same input in the following
explanation.

163
00:12:43,133 --> 00:12:46,833
Different component signs represented the same
final orientation.

164
00:12:47,193 --> 00:12:50,673
Can that orientation reveal the preceding
motion?

165
00:12:51,093 --> 00:12:53,673
Let us follow one interval through time.

166
00:12:54,300 --> 00:12:58,560
Follow the body forward direction and ski line
through the airborne rotation.

167
00:13:03,891 --> 00:13:09,111
A single frame cannot reveal the full movement
that preceded it.

168
00:13:14,561 --> 00:13:18,141
Time reveals the process; endpoint orientation

169
00:13:18,381 --> 00:13:20,421
and movement history are different
information.

170
00:13:25,752 --> 00:13:30,772
Connect this distinction to the two signed
quaternions representing one orientation.

171
00:13:30,972 --> 00:13:45,733
One quaternion does not automatically retain
the number of visible revolutions.

172
00:13:45,733 --> 00:13:47,753
If a trick needs accumulated rotation,

173
00:13:47,753 --> 00:13:51,653
track input or rotation history separately
from orientation.

174
00:13:52,533 --> 00:13:55,173
An endpoint pose did not store every earlier
action.

175
00:13:56,113 --> 00:13:58,573
Observe the torso and wheels during snow
travel.

176
00:13:59,613 --> 00:14:01,813
The red line shows the torso direction.

177
00:14:02,913 --> 00:14:05,213
The blue line shows a visible wheel direction.

178
00:14:06,333 --> 00:14:09,553
Both directions change through slopes and
bends.

179
00:14:10,593 --> 00:14:14,393
Identity rotation does not mean that the whole
game image stops.

180
00:14:15,353 --> 00:14:18,173
It applies no extra rotation to an input
direction.

181
00:14:19,073 --> 00:14:20,993
Treat the torso direction as the input.

182
00:14:21,973 --> 00:14:24,913
Which four components return it unchanged?

183
00:14:25,533 --> 00:14:31,133
Final orientation and rotation history are
different information. We now need a value

184
00:14:31,373 --> 00:14:35,193
that adds no rotation, and will check why
initializing every component to zero fails.

185
00:14:35,817 --> 00:14:42,517
The multiplication identity is [1,0,0,0];
multiplying q by it leaves q unchanged.

186
00:14:43,437 --> 00:14:50,357
[-1,0,0,0] is also the identity rotation, but
is not the algebraic multiplication identity.

187
00:14:50,837 --> 00:14:55,917
It changes q to -q: different components
representing the same orientation.

188
00:14:56,397 --> 00:15:04,397
An all-zero quaternion is not no rotation; it
is a zero-norm invalid rotation input.

189
00:15:04,397 --> 00:15:08,817
Initialize with the library identity
constructor

190
00:15:08,817 --> 00:15:11,777
or correctly ordered unit components.

191
00:15:12,237 --> 00:15:17,877
Distinguish equality as numbers from equality
as rotations before reasoning about signs.

192
00:15:18,500 --> 00:15:23,680
The identity value had length one. Other
stored rotations use that same convention,

193
00:15:24,060 --> 00:15:28,660
so we need to detect drift caused by repeated
calculations.

194
00:15:30,067 --> 00:15:32,887
The norm is the square root of the sum of all
four

195
00:15:33,107 --> 00:15:35,407
squared components, not just the vector norm.

196
00:15:35,607 --> 00:15:38,227
Typical orientation storage and

197
00:15:38,227 --> 00:15:40,047
quaternion-to-matrix routines expect unit
inputs.

198
00:15:40,387 --> 00:15:45,687
For small floating-point drift, divide all
four components by the norm.

199
00:15:45,867 --> 00:15:49,447
Guard near-zero and nonfinite norms before
division;

200
00:15:49,663 --> 00:15:52,867
do not disguise arbitrary invalid data as a
valid rotation.

201
00:15:53,167 --> 00:15:58,287
A nonzero nonunit quaternion can also rotate
with its correct

202
00:15:58,567 --> 00:16:01,227
general inverse because scale cancels, unlike
unit-assuming code.

203
00:16:01,727 --> 00:16:05,427
Normalization restores the scale convention.

204
00:16:05,427 --> 00:16:08,847
It neither scales the rotation angle directly
nor fixes wrong axes or multiplication order.

205
00:16:09,467 --> 00:16:14,077
Normalization means changing the norm to one.
For [3,0,0,4],

206
00:16:14,077 --> 00:16:19,407
the norm is the square root of three squared
plus four squared, which is five.

207
00:16:19,584 --> 00:16:25,567
Divide all four components by five. The scalar
becomes 0.6, both x and y remain zero,

208
00:16:25,567 --> 00:16:31,287
and z becomes 0.8. The squared norm is 0.36
plus 0.64, which equals one.

209
00:16:31,467 --> 00:16:36,287
The numerical magnitude changes while the axis
and component ratios remain.

210
00:16:36,287 --> 00:16:40,207
This does not correct a wrongly selected axis
or multiplication order.

211
00:16:40,627 --> 00:16:45,627
Likewise, faster character motion does not
require a larger orientation quaternion norm.

212
00:16:45,987 --> 00:16:48,527
We will observe speed and orientation
separately in the game.

213
00:16:49,433 --> 00:16:51,313
The rotation quaternion had norm one.

214
00:16:51,313 --> 00:16:59,767
Separate the displayed speed from the visible
body direction.

215
00:16:59,767 --> 00:17:04,354
This is a separate flight excerpt.

216
00:17:04,354 --> 00:17:06,314
The red wing line shows orientation; the
moving position shows a route.

217
00:17:06,314 --> 00:17:11,922
A larger speed display does not imply a larger
unit-rotation norm.

218
00:17:13,750 --> 00:17:15,370
Next, the equipment changes to a bicycle.
Apparent size and speed differ,

219
00:17:15,370 --> 00:17:23,113
while the unit-length condition remains the
same.

220
00:17:23,733 --> 00:17:29,073
We have checked normalization. To avoid
thinking that faster movement needs

221
00:17:29,073 --> 00:17:34,113
a longer quaternion, we will observe speed and
orientation separately.

222
00:17:34,733 --> 00:17:37,773
We will move from skiing to a flight segment.

223
00:17:38,053 --> 00:17:40,393
Watch movement speed separately from body
orientation.

224
00:17:42,945 --> 00:17:48,785
Faster motion does not mean a longer
quaternion; a unit rotation retains norm one.

225
00:17:49,145 --> 00:17:54,617
Speed and acceleration describe change,

226
00:17:54,777 --> 00:17:56,377
whereas orientation describes the current
state.

227
00:17:56,717 --> 00:18:01,309
The following camera moves as the wings bank.

228
00:18:01,909 --> 00:18:05,269
Apparent image size cannot reveal quaternion
norm.

229
00:18:07,981 --> 00:18:14,821
Check unit length in code or data; this
footage is not a measurement of those values.

230
00:18:17,613 --> 00:18:22,633
After observing motion, we will calculate how
to undo a stored rotation in the next diagram.

231
00:18:23,733 --> 00:18:29,225
Riding and flight are separate excerpts. Read
speed and orientation separately.

232
00:18:30,717 --> 00:18:34,677
During flight, read the red wing tilt
separately from the speed display.

233
00:18:38,957 --> 00:18:47,797
Which components change to undo our unit
rotation? We now calculate with the conjugate.

234
00:18:48,517 --> 00:18:50,357
The component is turned sideways and upright.

235
00:18:55,410 --> 00:18:57,310
Inspect its other faces before calling the
full orientation identical.

236
00:18:58,050 --> 00:19:00,710
Standing upright alone does not establish an
identical full orientation.

237
00:19:00,930 --> 00:19:06,514
We verify an exact inverse using the defined
model. Define the initial

238
00:19:06,514 --> 00:19:10,310
model direction as one, zero, zero. A
ninety-degree z-axis turn gives zero,

239
00:19:10,310 --> 00:19:14,410
one, zero; the opposite ninety-degree turn
restores one, zero, zero.

240
00:19:14,870 --> 00:19:20,630
The cancelling rotation is the inverse. It
does not erase placement or traveled motion.

241
00:19:20,750 --> 00:19:25,190
These are defined, checkable model angles, not
measurements from the footage.

242
00:19:25,817 --> 00:19:32,937
Orientation and speed were separate
quantities. Let us now undo a stored rotation;

243
00:19:33,177 --> 00:19:36,837
the unit-length condition we maintained makes
this calculation simpler.

244
00:19:37,737 --> 00:19:42,147
Conjugation keeps the scalar and negates the
three vector components.

245
00:19:42,547 --> 00:19:48,467
For a unit rotation quaternion, the conjugate
is the inverse and undoes the rotation.

246
00:19:48,727 --> 00:19:53,107
Conjugating z90 preserves w and negates z,

247
00:19:53,467 --> 00:19:55,667
giving z minus90.

248
00:19:55,827 --> 00:19:59,467
Their product returns [1,0,0,0].

249
00:19:59,557 --> 00:20:06,447
Negating all four components instead gives the
same orientation, not generally the inverse.

250
00:20:06,447 --> 00:20:10,667
Special cases such as half-turns have matching
inverse orientations,

251
00:20:10,667 --> 00:20:13,887
but conjugation and full negation remain
different operations.

252
00:20:14,550 --> 00:20:16,850
We inverted a unit rotation with its
conjugate.

253
00:20:17,010 --> 00:20:22,041
Separate the red torso line from the traveled
position.

254
00:20:24,373 --> 00:20:26,693
Even as the bank becomes smaller, the bicycle
continues forward.

255
00:20:28,984 --> 00:20:30,964
Rotation and translation are different
changes.

256
00:20:33,216 --> 00:20:40,567
An inverse cancels the defined rotation. It
does not rewind elapsed time or distance.

257
00:20:42,859 --> 00:20:45,819
Would conjugation alone invert a tuple whose
norm is not one?

258
00:20:48,070 --> 00:20:49,930
We verify that condition next.

259
00:20:50,550 --> 00:20:55,670
Conjugation undid a unit quaternion. Does the
same formula work when its length is not one?

260
00:20:55,670 --> 00:21:00,130
A small numerical example will check whether
their product is the identity.

261
00:21:00,750 --> 00:21:06,610
The general inverse is the conjugate divided
by the squared norm, not the norm itself.

262
00:21:06,870 --> 00:21:10,950
For [2,0,0,0], the norm is two and its square
is four.

263
00:21:11,210 --> 00:21:16,410
Dividing the conjugate by four gives
[0.5,0,0,0].

264
00:21:16,710 --> 00:21:18,530
Their product is the identity. Dividing by two
would

265
00:21:18,650 --> 00:21:21,850
leave scalar two in the product and fail the
inverse test.

266
00:21:21,990 --> 00:21:26,030
For unit inputs the squared norm is one,
recovering the conjugate rule.

267
00:21:26,330 --> 00:21:28,930
The inverse requires a nonzero quaternion;

268
00:21:28,942 --> 00:21:32,730
reject zero and nonfinite inputs and inspect
their origin.

269
00:21:33,433 --> 00:21:40,532
An inverse cancelled rotation. Read the red
torso line separately from traveled position.

270
00:21:43,391 --> 00:21:49,530
How do we connect two rotations? The next
episode verifies their product.

271
00:21:50,150 --> 00:21:53,830
We have connected the four components to a
computational

272
00:21:53,830 --> 00:21:56,490
representation of axis and angle, rather than
four spatial directions.

273
00:21:56,790 --> 00:22:02,530
Starting from a complex planar turn, we
constructed a quaternion with a unit axis and

274
00:22:02,930 --> 00:22:06,430
half-angle, checked its norm, and verified
that an inverse cancels the applied operation.

275
00:22:06,850 --> 00:22:09,710
A game orientation rarely stops after one
turn.

276
00:22:10,010 --> 00:22:13,750
The next episode explains why the order of two
turns matters and

277
00:22:13,750 --> 00:22:18,450
calculates the extra rotation from a current
orientation to a target.
